This challenge will give me more practice with Elastic. I definitely need more practice with the queries. 

The background for this challenge is using logs to investigate an alert from the IDS system. There are signs of C2 communication. The source of the communication is from a user in the HR department.

The month of interest was March 2022. To start off I did some basic analysis. This included seeing how many logs there were for the time span. After adjusting the time window, I found 1482 logs.

Next, I tried to find the associated IP address with the attack. On the left hand side, I just clicked on the source_ip filter. I assumed the one with the highest count was the associated address, which was correct.

We were told that the attacker used a legitimate binary to download a file from the C2 server. To find the name of this binary, I filtered by the relevant source_ip. Then within the user_agent field, I saw the binary.

Following a similar pattern, the task shared that the infected machine used a filesharing website. Within the results, filtered by source_ip, I was able to find the site by looking at the host field value. Within the log entry, I scrolled down to the uri section, which allowed me to get the full link.

Then I had to find the actual file that was downloaded. Using the link that I just found, I visited the website. This is usually bad to do, but since it is for learning purposes and within a VM, I felt completely fine doing this.

I could see the challenge flag displayed clearly once I visited the website. This challenge was shorter and a bit easier, but let me practice analyzing Elastic logs, since they are formatted a little differently than Splunk.

